  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe storms possible today with damaging wind \ngusts being the primary threat.\n\nAs the morning wears on, a potent cold front will steadily \napproach the region, entirely reshaping the day's weather. A \nwarm, humid, and highly unstable air mass will settle over the \nlandscape, characterized by steep lapse rates that will act as \nfuel for atmospheric trouble. By this afternoon and lasting into\nthe early evening, these volatile conditions will promote the \ndevelopment of strong to severe storms, placing most of the area\nunder a Marginal Risk for severe weather.\n\nThe environment will grow even more hostile further to the \nnortheast. Beyond a line stretching from Hot Springs to \nLynchburg, convective available potential energy (CAPE) and \nvertical wind shear will be notably higher. This potent \ncombination will support a Slight Risk zone, where more \norganized and longer-lived severe thunderstorms are expected to \nflourish. While a variety of hazardous weather is possible, \ntodays primary severe threat will manifest as damaging winds \ncapable of downing trees and power lines as the line of storms \nmarches through.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: There is a flash flood risk today.\n\nThe relentless wet pattern is about to intensify. Rain has \nalready been plentiful over the last seven days, but over the \nlast couple of days, the areal coverage of 3 to 5 inch rain \nrates has been steadily increasing. Some areas on either side of\nthe Blue Ridge south of Roanoke have already been primed, \nhaving swallowed two to four inches of rain in just the past 48 \nhours.\n\nWith precipitable water (PWAT) values hovering in the 1.5 to \n2.0- inch range today, the atmosphere will remain completely \nsaturated. As more organized storms begin to form and move \nthrough this afternoon, the chances for flash flooding will \nincrease across the entire area. Because the ground is already \nwet, the region will sit under a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall, meaning any heavy downpour could quickly translate \ninto rapid runoff and localized flooding.",
